WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:13.950
&gt;&gt; All right, welcome Cynthia to

00:00:13.951 --> 00:00:16.530
Microsoft Research
as part of your,

00:00:16.531 --> 00:00:20.385
the talk that you're going
to give in the next hour.

00:00:20.386 --> 00:00:23.525
So, a pleasure to have you here.

00:00:23.526 --> 00:00:24.085
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:00:24.086 --> 00:00:29.415
&gt;&gt; To just get started
with, I'd love to,

00:00:29.416 --> 00:00:31.590
you know, what got you
started in the whole

00:00:31.591 --> 00:00:35.190
area of robotics, especially
in the social robotics.

00:00:35.191 --> 00:00:35.645
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:00:35.646 --> 00:00:37.810
&gt;&gt; What were the biggest
motivations and inspirations?

00:00:37.811 --> 00:00:39.980
Tell us about your initial part
of your journey.

00:00:39.981 --> 00:00:41.870
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so I think,

00:00:41.871 --> 00:00:46.735
looking back, I can tell
a very linear story.

00:00:46.736 --> 00:00:48.390
When you're living life,

00:00:48.391 --> 00:00:49.830
it's really not that way.

00:00:49.831 --> 00:00:52.030
I mean, you go on
various branches of

00:00:52.031 --> 00:00:55.050
your life's path and you
end up where you end up,

00:00:55.051 --> 00:00:57.000
which has been
an incredible journey.

00:00:57.001 --> 00:00:59.930
But for me, I mean, I do
think it really started,

00:00:59.931 --> 00:01:04.800
I was 10 and the first
Star Wars movie came out.

00:01:04.801 --> 00:01:07.375
I think today,

00:01:07.376 --> 00:01:10.270
kids today don't understand
what a phenomenon that was.

00:01:10.271 --> 00:01:12.970
It's like, I remember sitting

00:01:12.971 --> 00:01:15.895
in the theater and the Imperial

00:01:15.896 --> 00:01:17.800
whatever galactic star
or whatever

00:01:17.801 --> 00:01:20.380
was going overhead, and
the seats were vibrating.

00:01:20.381 --> 00:01:24.190
It was just, like,
this awe-inspiring experience

00:01:24.191 --> 00:01:25.360
from the very beginning and then,

00:01:25.361 --> 00:01:27.820
these droids came on the screen

00:01:27.821 --> 00:01:31.360
and I just fell in
love with them.

00:01:31.361 --> 00:01:33.010
I think it's because they

00:01:33.011 --> 00:01:36.430
weren't just robots,
like useful robots,

00:01:36.431 --> 00:01:37.990
but it was really because

00:01:37.991 --> 00:01:39.790
they were full-fledged
characters.

00:01:39.791 --> 00:01:41.865
They had rich personalities.

00:01:41.866 --> 00:01:44.620
They had relationships
not only with each

00:01:44.621 --> 00:01:47.470
other, but with
the main human characters,

00:01:47.471 --> 00:01:50.840
and they really cared
about one another.

00:01:50.841 --> 00:01:55.570
I thought that emotional
connection really stuck with me,

00:01:55.571 --> 00:01:58.960
and I think the whole world
fell in love with

00:01:58.961 --> 00:02:00.525
those robots for a reason.

00:02:00.526 --> 00:02:03.520
So fast forward,

00:02:03.521 --> 00:02:08.050
I ended up doing
my graduate work at

00:02:08.051 --> 00:02:11.655
the MIT Artificial Intelligence
Lab, before it was CSAIL,

00:02:11.656 --> 00:02:14.740
where Professor Rod
Brooks was my adviser and

00:02:14.741 --> 00:02:17.840
he's recognized as the father
of autonomous robots.

00:02:17.841 --> 00:02:21.490
I remember when I was admitted

00:02:21.491 --> 00:02:22.990
as a graduate student
and looking at

00:02:22.991 --> 00:02:25.400
the various schools and
deciding where I should go,

00:02:25.401 --> 00:02:28.110
and I walked into
Rod Brooks' lab,

00:02:28.111 --> 00:02:31.255
which was then called
the Mobile Robotics Group.

00:02:31.256 --> 00:02:35.320
I saw all these little
insect inspired robots,

00:02:35.321 --> 00:02:36.430
and a lot of the work that was

00:02:36.431 --> 00:02:39.630
around biological
inspiration intelligence.

00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:43.760
I remember just
thinking, "Oh, my God!

00:02:43.761 --> 00:02:46.195
If those robots and Star Wars

00:02:46.196 --> 00:02:49.160
are ever going to really
become a reality,

00:02:49.161 --> 00:02:51.930
it's going to star in
a lab just like this.

00:02:51.931 --> 00:02:53.084
In fact, maybe this lab."

00:02:53.085 --> 00:02:55.100
And I'm like, "I
have to be here."

00:02:55.101 --> 00:02:57.010
So that was really it.

00:02:57.011 --> 00:03:00.970
It was the promise of robots
as artificial creatures,

00:03:00.971 --> 00:03:04.090
back then, very
inspired by insects,

00:03:04.091 --> 00:03:08.380
But then you could imagine
the evolution of that.

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:12.230
We thought, we would
go from insects,

00:03:12.231 --> 00:03:14.050
to like reptiles to like I

00:03:14.051 --> 00:03:16.100
don't know mammals or whatever
then eventually maybe,

00:03:16.101 --> 00:03:17.630
eventually maybe
you get to people.

00:03:17.631 --> 00:03:21.260
But I remember when Rod
went on sabbatical,

00:03:21.261 --> 00:03:23.620
he did this world tour.

00:03:23.621 --> 00:03:24.870
He came back and he's
like, "We're doing

00:03:24.871 --> 00:03:26.740
humanoids." We're like, "What?"

00:03:26.741 --> 00:03:28.215
It's like we just got done

00:03:28.216 --> 00:03:29.880
doing insects, we're
doing humanoids.

00:03:29.881 --> 00:03:33.100
It's because he spent time
as part of that in Japan,

00:03:33.101 --> 00:03:35.440
and he had seen all of
this humanoid work.

00:03:35.441 --> 00:03:38.500
You know back then,
the reason why

00:03:38.501 --> 00:03:41.830
humanoid robotics was
so important in Japan

00:03:41.831 --> 00:03:43.660
was just they were
already grappling

00:03:43.661 --> 00:03:45.560
with this profound shift, right,

00:03:45.561 --> 00:03:47.295
where the ratio of
working-age people

00:03:47.296 --> 00:03:49.660
to retired-age persons

00:03:49.661 --> 00:03:50.830
was going to hit

00:03:50.831 --> 00:03:52.900
their country harder than
many other countries.

00:03:52.901 --> 00:03:55.120
They were already thinking
about robots as a way

00:03:55.121 --> 00:03:57.595
of helping to bridge that gap.

00:03:57.596 --> 00:03:59.950
But humanoid was,
because so much of

00:03:59.951 --> 00:04:03.720
our human engineered space,
was about this morphology,

00:04:03.721 --> 00:04:04.940
walking around, tools,

00:04:04.941 --> 00:04:06.410
everything was built
for this morphology.

00:04:06.411 --> 00:04:09.030
So that's why you
wanted humanoids.

00:04:09.310 --> 00:04:14.030
I remember the day
that I was still a

00:04:14.031 --> 00:04:19.005
graduate student, NASA
landed robots on Mars.

00:04:19.006 --> 00:04:21.220
I remember thinking,
because then,

00:04:21.221 --> 00:04:22.900
the value proposition of autonomy

00:04:22.901 --> 00:04:24.160
was about the dull, dirty, and

00:04:24.161 --> 00:04:25.625
dangerous far from people, right?

00:04:25.626 --> 00:04:27.280
That was why you did
autonomous robots.

00:04:27.281 --> 00:04:29.055
I remember thinking to myself,

00:04:29.056 --> 00:04:32.590
"All right, here we are. We
send robots into the oceans,

00:04:32.591 --> 00:04:34.185
we send them into
volcanoes, and now, we

00:04:34.186 --> 00:04:36.100
even land them on Mars.

00:04:36.101 --> 00:04:38.920
Why aren't they in
our living rooms?"

00:04:38.921 --> 00:04:41.770
It was that moment that
I'm like, "It's because

00:04:41.771 --> 00:04:45.360
designing a machine
and intelligence

00:04:45.361 --> 00:04:48.005
that can deal with
the inanimate world,

00:04:48.006 --> 00:04:50.280
navigating around rocks on Mars

00:04:50.281 --> 00:04:52.685
is fundamentally different than

00:04:52.686 --> 00:04:54.940
designing an intelligent
machine that has

00:04:54.941 --> 00:04:57.085
to interact with entities,

00:04:57.086 --> 00:04:59.655
whose behavior is governed
by having a mind."

00:04:59.656 --> 00:05:01.635
That was totally different.

00:05:01.636 --> 00:05:03.370
Nobody was really working on

00:05:03.371 --> 00:05:06.820
that problem of robots
for the rest of us.

00:05:06.821 --> 00:05:08.650
It was always autonomous robots

00:05:08.651 --> 00:05:10.675
as tools for professionals.

00:05:10.676 --> 00:05:12.910
Nobody was really thinking about

00:05:12.911 --> 00:05:15.100
autonomous robots in
people's homes, where

00:05:15.101 --> 00:05:17.770
young children to seniors and
everyone in between would

00:05:17.771 --> 00:05:18.940
be able to interact and get

00:05:18.941 --> 00:05:20.455
value from this kind
of technology.

00:05:20.456 --> 00:05:21.760
So that was the moment,

00:05:21.761 --> 00:05:23.580
and I remember I marched

00:05:23.581 --> 00:05:25.870
into poor Rod's office
and I'm like,

00:05:25.871 --> 00:05:27.530
"I have to change it, everything.

00:05:27.531 --> 00:05:30.400
I [inaudible] my PhD,
I'm changing it.

00:05:30.401 --> 00:05:32.585
I'm doing social human
robot interaction."

00:05:32.586 --> 00:05:34.030
That's when I started
building Kismet,

00:05:34.031 --> 00:05:37.045
and he, I mean to
Rod's credit, he was, like,

00:05:37.046 --> 00:05:39.860
"That's an important
problem. Go do it."

00:05:39.861 --> 00:05:40.960
Now, you might have thought he

00:05:40.961 --> 00:05:42.010
might have been all freaked out

00:05:42.011 --> 00:05:44.589
having a student say, "I
have to change everything,"

00:05:44.590 --> 00:05:48.070
but he was always so supportive.

00:05:48.071 --> 00:05:49.720
I mean, I think one of
his greatest strengths,

00:05:49.721 --> 00:05:52.145
he was always a visionary.

00:05:52.146 --> 00:05:53.860
Yeah. You could tell when

00:05:53.861 --> 00:05:55.710
there was a problem that
was really worth pursuing.

00:05:55.711 --> 00:05:57.340
So anyway, I'm very fortunate

00:05:57.341 --> 00:06:00.785
to have been a student of

00:06:00.786 --> 00:06:02.110
Rod Brooks because he gave me

00:06:02.111 --> 00:06:05.010
the freedom to do something
crazy like Kismet,

00:06:05.011 --> 00:06:08.380
which was around
the social emotional

00:06:08.381 --> 00:06:10.300
face-to-face interaction
with robots.

00:06:10.301 --> 00:06:12.625
That was considered kind
of crazy at the time.

00:06:12.626 --> 00:06:14.185
&gt;&gt; Yeah. Very inspiring.

00:06:14.186 --> 00:06:18.405
I mean, having some time
spent at MIT as well, I've

00:06:18.406 --> 00:06:20.890
realized that sometimes you

00:06:20.891 --> 00:06:22.990
have these ideas,
which might feel

00:06:22.991 --> 00:06:25.150
so out of the box or difficult.

00:06:25.151 --> 00:06:27.835
So for instance,
when we think about

00:06:27.836 --> 00:06:30.455
these robots that would
be around inanimate

00:06:30.456 --> 00:06:33.275
objects like spiders,
around rocks.

00:06:33.276 --> 00:06:36.100
If you think about it, we
know the laws of motion,

00:06:36.101 --> 00:06:39.370
the laws of physics for
almost 300 years now.

00:06:39.371 --> 00:06:42.195
Even when we know those laws,

00:06:42.196 --> 00:06:44.185
building something
is more difficult.

00:06:44.186 --> 00:06:47.315
Now, you're getting into
the realm of social robotics,

00:06:47.316 --> 00:06:49.420
where we don't have
these laws well-defined.

00:06:49.421 --> 00:06:52.260
We are still studying it.
Don't think it was kind of

00:06:52.261 --> 00:06:53.860
crazy, and what do you think,

00:06:53.861 --> 00:06:56.395
given the difficulty
of this challenge,

00:06:56.396 --> 00:06:58.645
what hope do we have
in accomplishing

00:06:58.646 --> 00:07:00.905
something like that in
the next five or ten years?

00:07:00.906 --> 00:07:02.570
What are your thoughts on this?

00:07:02.571 --> 00:07:03.610
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So I think, I mean,

00:07:03.611 --> 00:07:06.030
certainly as a graduate student,

00:07:06.031 --> 00:07:08.110
what we had known already

00:07:08.111 --> 00:07:11.095
from building these
autonomous robots was that

00:07:11.096 --> 00:07:13.690
people had a very
powerful tendency

00:07:13.691 --> 00:07:15.490
to want to anthropomorphize
them, right?

00:07:15.491 --> 00:07:16.840
They didn't only
think about them as

00:07:16.841 --> 00:07:18.205
kind of useful tools.

00:07:18.206 --> 00:07:20.410
I mean they really, applied what

00:07:20.411 --> 00:07:22.840
we would call our kind
of social thinking,

00:07:22.841 --> 00:07:24.760
theory of other minds
and so forth, to try

00:07:24.761 --> 00:07:27.725
to reason about and make sense
of the robots' behavior.

00:07:27.726 --> 00:07:32.770
So, one of the key
starting hypotheses was if this

00:07:32.771 --> 00:07:35.110
is how the human mind
naturally is trying to make

00:07:35.111 --> 00:07:37.750
sense of this
autonomous-behaving entity,

00:07:37.751 --> 00:07:39.340
then what we need to
do is understand that

00:07:39.341 --> 00:07:40.990
and designed to
support the way that

00:07:40.991 --> 00:07:42.400
people want to think about and

00:07:42.401 --> 00:07:44.260
understand and collaborate
with these technologies.

00:07:44.261 --> 00:07:45.280
So, it was always a very

00:07:45.281 --> 00:07:49.396
human-centered,
human-driven process.

00:07:49.397 --> 00:07:52.240
The other thing, of
course, is that I

00:07:52.241 --> 00:07:54.320
recognize that trying to model

00:07:54.321 --> 00:07:57.315
adult human-human
interaction was like,

00:07:57.316 --> 00:08:00.490
that's crazy because we
are the most socially,

00:08:00.491 --> 00:08:02.905
emotionally sophisticated
species on the planet.

00:08:02.906 --> 00:08:04.225
So the thought was,

00:08:04.226 --> 00:08:08.890
"What is the simplest
social other that I could

00:08:08.891 --> 00:08:13.940
model that adults would still
treat as a social entity?"

00:08:13.941 --> 00:08:18.035
So, a lot of that work with
Kismet was really inspired by

00:08:18.036 --> 00:08:23.629
social development of
infant-caregiver interactions.

00:08:23.630 --> 00:08:27.720
It's so much of the developmental
psychology is about.

00:08:27.721 --> 00:08:30.925
The critical roles that
we, as the caring,

00:08:30.926 --> 00:08:34.910
nurturing adults, play
in treating the infant,

00:08:34.911 --> 00:08:36.020
treating the baby as

00:08:36.021 --> 00:08:39.320
though he or she is fully
socially intelligent and

00:08:39.321 --> 00:08:42.195
capable because it's
those interactions that are

00:08:42.196 --> 00:08:45.295
needed in order for the child
to learn how to be calm.

00:08:45.296 --> 00:08:46.745
So you treat as though,

00:08:46.746 --> 00:08:48.350
so they learn to be calm.

00:08:48.351 --> 00:08:51.490
So, that was the paradigm
behind Kismet.

00:08:51.491 --> 00:08:54.140
So, it was all about
this very first

00:08:54.141 --> 00:08:55.540
face-to-face social
emotional work,

00:08:55.541 --> 00:08:57.765
where our communication starts.

00:08:57.766 --> 00:08:59.670
It grows and develops over time.

00:08:59.671 --> 00:09:03.430
So that's kind of how I
started, just appreciating.

00:09:03.431 --> 00:09:06.890
You got to start in
the primordial aspect of this,

00:09:06.891 --> 00:09:11.160
but understanding and learning
so much and just not about

00:09:11.161 --> 00:09:13.855
emotion and social processes and

00:09:13.856 --> 00:09:16.380
theory of other mind
processes on a machine.

00:09:16.381 --> 00:09:20.355
I mean, that's like
whole new areas of AI now,

00:09:20.356 --> 00:09:22.885
but also starting to appreciate

00:09:22.886 --> 00:09:25.580
the human side of
that partnership.

00:09:25.581 --> 00:09:27.440
So in reading that literature,

00:09:27.441 --> 00:09:29.360
I contrast it so much today,

00:09:29.361 --> 00:09:31.750
where so many of the AIs
we interact with,

00:09:31.751 --> 00:09:33.445
they feel very transactional,

00:09:33.446 --> 00:09:34.940
they feel very much
like playing chess.

00:09:34.941 --> 00:09:36.510
I say something,
machine responds.

00:09:36.511 --> 00:09:38.085
I say something,
machine responds.

00:09:38.086 --> 00:09:39.800
Communication is not like that.

00:09:39.801 --> 00:09:41.970
Human communication
is nothing like that.

00:09:41.971 --> 00:09:44.235
It's a dance. Often and a
lot of this literature,

00:09:44.236 --> 00:09:46.275
they talk about
the mother-infant dance,

00:09:46.276 --> 00:09:47.730
and it's because the way we

00:09:47.731 --> 00:09:49.250
communicate and collaborate is

00:09:49.251 --> 00:09:54.840
so dynamic and interwoven
and mutually regulating.

00:09:54.841 --> 00:09:56.845
That's what we do
in order to really

00:09:56.846 --> 00:09:58.950
coordinate states of
mind and behavior.

00:09:58.951 --> 00:10:02.500
It's obvious to me at
the time that that was one of

00:10:02.501 --> 00:10:04.460
the biggest challenges
of creating

00:10:04.461 --> 00:10:06.620
this kind of socially
collaborative technology.

00:10:06.621 --> 00:10:10.045
It was getting the nuances
of that dance right,

00:10:10.046 --> 00:10:13.285
and it's richly non-verbal
in addition to being verbal,

00:10:13.286 --> 00:10:14.965
in addition to just inferring

00:10:14.966 --> 00:10:17.055
this invisible states of mind,

00:10:17.056 --> 00:10:20.480
beliefs, intense desires from
watching human behavior.

00:10:20.481 --> 00:10:23.890
I mean, again, so I think

00:10:23.891 --> 00:10:25.320
one of the examples
is that because

00:10:25.321 --> 00:10:26.945
we as humans do it
so effortlessly,

00:10:26.946 --> 00:10:28.490
we assume it's easier
than it really is.

00:10:28.491 --> 00:10:30.390
I think it's one of the
most sophisticated,

00:10:30.391 --> 00:10:32.410
hardest AI challenges facing

00:10:32.411 --> 00:10:33.995
us is understanding how to create

00:10:33.996 --> 00:10:37.035
these robots or intelligent
machines that can actually

00:10:37.036 --> 00:10:40.425
really collaborate
with us as others.

00:10:40.426 --> 00:10:43.535
So sophisticated and
complex and nuanced.

00:10:43.536 --> 00:10:46.900
&gt;&gt; Yeah. Actually, it blows
my mind when I think about

00:10:46.901 --> 00:10:48.540
the complexities
when I'm interacting

00:10:48.541 --> 00:10:50.865
with my two-year-old
or five-year-old.

00:10:50.866 --> 00:10:52.920
How sophisticated, how adaptive

00:10:52.921 --> 00:10:54.380
they are and how
quickly they learn,

00:10:54.381 --> 00:11:00.460
and you have to almost keep
at phase as they are growing.

00:11:00.461 --> 00:11:03.470
If you look at the landscape of

00:11:03.471 --> 00:11:07.245
the recent advances in
machine learning and AI,

00:11:07.246 --> 00:11:09.985
you'll see that many
of these successes

00:11:09.986 --> 00:11:12.960
are data heavy, deep
machine learning,

00:11:12.961 --> 00:11:15.355
hundreds and millions
of data points,

00:11:15.356 --> 00:11:18.695
and trials, and images,
and sound recordings.

00:11:18.696 --> 00:11:20.890
Given the complexity of

00:11:20.891 --> 00:11:23.190
the problem that you
just described, I mean,

00:11:23.191 --> 00:11:25.865
can you comment on is data

00:11:25.866 --> 00:11:29.095
the way to go there, or are
there other mechanisms that

00:11:29.096 --> 00:11:31.720
we should think about that
goes beyond the realm of just

00:11:31.721 --> 00:11:34.020
collecting or doing some kind

00:11:34.021 --> 00:11:35.330
of reinforcement learning,
for instance?

00:11:35.331 --> 00:11:38.210
&gt;&gt; Sure. I mean, I think
the punch line is way more.

00:11:38.211 --> 00:11:41.325
So, even, again, when I

00:11:41.326 --> 00:11:42.600
started reading and starting

00:11:42.601 --> 00:11:44.190
a lot of the infant psychology,

00:11:44.191 --> 00:11:46.315
it was very apparent
that even despite all of

00:11:46.316 --> 00:11:48.795
our evolutionary advantages
and endowments,

00:11:48.796 --> 00:11:50.500
when we are born into the world,

00:11:50.501 --> 00:11:52.725
there's a lot of
machinery up there,

00:11:52.726 --> 00:11:53.990
it's not just data.

00:11:53.991 --> 00:11:55.210
It's a lot of machinery,

00:11:55.211 --> 00:11:57.065
a lot of structure that

00:11:57.066 --> 00:11:59.350
when we gain the
experience from the world,

00:11:59.351 --> 00:12:00.920
that's what allows us to actually

00:12:00.921 --> 00:12:02.325
learn the right kinds of things.

00:12:02.326 --> 00:12:06.295
So, it's got to be a combination
of the right cognitive,

00:12:06.296 --> 00:12:07.670
emotional, social kind of

00:12:07.671 --> 00:12:10.640
mechanisms married
with the experience.

00:12:10.641 --> 00:12:12.990
That is what really
makes this happen.

00:12:12.991 --> 00:12:16.200
Of course, we also know
that in tragic situations

00:12:16.201 --> 00:12:19.495
where infants don't receive
enough social interaction,

00:12:19.496 --> 00:12:22.730
they become irrevocably,
severely retarded.

00:12:22.731 --> 00:12:24.370
So even again for ourselves,

00:12:24.371 --> 00:12:26.110
if we don't get
the right interaction

00:12:26.111 --> 00:12:27.605
with others in the social world,

00:12:27.606 --> 00:12:30.455
we need the kind of scaffolding

00:12:30.456 --> 00:12:35.875
of empathetic other to help
us learn on the right path.

00:12:35.876 --> 00:12:39.715
So, I think that was just
again very eye-opening for me

00:12:39.716 --> 00:12:43.715
and just very cognizant as
we try to create these AI.

00:12:43.716 --> 00:12:45.390
So, deep learning, of course, has

00:12:45.391 --> 00:12:48.855
been hugely successful
and there's still a lot,

00:12:48.856 --> 00:12:51.135
I guess, a lot of runway
for deep learning.

00:12:51.136 --> 00:12:54.130
But it is about
large data sets and

00:12:54.131 --> 00:12:55.455
pre-labeled data sets, which is

00:12:55.456 --> 00:12:57.825
very labor intensive to create.

00:12:57.826 --> 00:13:00.145
There is a lot of data

00:13:00.146 --> 00:13:02.290
that doesn't readily
exist right now.

00:13:02.291 --> 00:13:05.170
So when you talk about this
interpersonal, mutual,

00:13:05.171 --> 00:13:07.965
regulated dynamics
of interaction,

00:13:07.966 --> 00:13:11.400
that has to be learned
from experience.

00:13:11.401 --> 00:13:13.120
There may be ways of

00:13:13.121 --> 00:13:15.680
simulating that to get
a bootstrap up on that,

00:13:15.681 --> 00:13:16.980
but human behavior, again, is

00:13:16.981 --> 00:13:19.410
so rich and nuanced and averse.

00:13:19.411 --> 00:13:22.470
You still, I think, you're
going to need real interaction

00:13:22.471 --> 00:13:23.960
in order to learn the right kinds

00:13:23.961 --> 00:13:25.550
of behaviors and mechanisms.

00:13:25.551 --> 00:13:27.565
So, I do think
reinforcement learning

00:13:27.566 --> 00:13:28.915
is going to be important.

00:13:28.916 --> 00:13:30.240
I think it's clear

00:13:30.241 --> 00:13:33.265
that we can also make
these inferences.

00:13:33.266 --> 00:13:37.160
So, the theory of other minds
sorts of challenges.

00:13:37.161 --> 00:13:40.005
I mean, it's ways of even

00:13:40.006 --> 00:13:44.390
simulating as a way of
reasoning and predicting,

00:13:44.391 --> 00:13:47.260
our other sets of
mechanisms that we use.

00:13:47.261 --> 00:13:49.890
Empathy, the ability to learn

00:13:49.891 --> 00:13:52.505
by watching others,
taking their perspective,

00:13:52.506 --> 00:13:53.920
using their experience as

00:13:53.921 --> 00:13:55.415
a surrogate for
your own experience as

00:13:55.416 --> 00:13:57.740
social learning is
a whole other suite

00:13:57.741 --> 00:13:59.980
of many different kinds of
social learning that we use.

00:13:59.981 --> 00:14:04.350
So, again, I think we have

00:14:04.351 --> 00:14:06.000
so many ways of
understanding the world and

00:14:06.001 --> 00:14:09.240
so many ways of learning from
experience that it's going

00:14:09.241 --> 00:14:12.845
to be an orchestra

00:14:12.846 --> 00:14:16.575
of these kinds of methods to
really get to the AI that

00:14:16.576 --> 00:14:20.245
we associate with being
like the socially,

00:14:20.246 --> 00:14:21.450
emotionally, intelligent
creature that

00:14:21.451 --> 00:14:23.175
we can collaborate with.

00:14:23.176 --> 00:14:25.610
So, we're just
scratching the surface.

00:14:25.611 --> 00:14:26.940
Just scratching the surface.

00:14:26.941 --> 00:14:28.825
&gt;&gt; Yeah, definitely. In fact,

00:14:28.826 --> 00:14:34.745
it reminds me of when you were
doing the work on Kismet,

00:14:34.746 --> 00:14:38.375
you had mentioned that
one of the reasons

00:14:38.376 --> 00:14:41.790
that Kismet doesn't talk,

00:14:41.791 --> 00:14:45.135
because it's depicting
the state of the technology.

00:14:45.136 --> 00:14:46.680
It's still in infancy,

00:14:46.681 --> 00:14:48.200
so it's appropriate for us to

00:14:48.201 --> 00:14:50.385
basically talk to an infant.

00:14:50.386 --> 00:14:53.450
It's been almost 20
years since that worked.

00:14:53.451 --> 00:14:55.565
What's your sense of
one of the things that

00:14:55.566 --> 00:14:57.560
have evolved? Are we

00:14:57.561 --> 00:14:59.290
still at the state of infancy,

00:14:59.291 --> 00:15:02.035
or has the technology matured?

00:15:02.036 --> 00:15:03.690
What's your take on that?

00:15:03.691 --> 00:15:06.820
&gt;&gt; So, I think

00:15:06.821 --> 00:15:10.645
the technology has advanced,
of course, a great deal,

00:15:10.646 --> 00:15:12.620
and yet, it still has
a long way to go.

00:15:12.621 --> 00:15:16.380
So, when we started with Kismet,

00:15:16.640 --> 00:15:19.750
it was very, very difficult
to be able to have

00:15:19.751 --> 00:15:22.705
robust ASR or automatic
speech recognition.

00:15:22.706 --> 00:15:26.180
The machine vision
was very primordial.

00:15:26.181 --> 00:15:28.285
I think one of

00:15:28.286 --> 00:15:30.650
the most exciting things
that's happened more recently

00:15:30.651 --> 00:15:31.990
is just the performance and

00:15:31.991 --> 00:15:35.190
affordability of
those core technologies,

00:15:35.191 --> 00:15:36.530
have advanced to the state,

00:15:36.531 --> 00:15:38.350
although they're
far from perfect,

00:15:38.351 --> 00:15:40.335
they're interesting
enough that you can

00:15:40.336 --> 00:15:43.769
start to create services,

00:15:43.770 --> 00:15:45.410
products, whatever
you want to call it,

00:15:45.411 --> 00:15:47.830
that actually are
interesting to people.

00:15:47.831 --> 00:15:50.150
They can certainly
get a lot better,

00:15:50.151 --> 00:15:51.610
a lot more sophisticated,

00:15:51.611 --> 00:15:53.920
but at least you can start to
build real systems that are

00:15:53.921 --> 00:15:56.550
actually able to provide
value in the world.

00:15:56.551 --> 00:15:58.160
In many ways, by doing that,

00:15:58.161 --> 00:16:00.410
just become painstakingly clear

00:16:00.411 --> 00:16:03.095
also about the opportunity
and the challenge that still

00:16:03.096 --> 00:16:06.880
lies ahead to really
have these systems meet

00:16:06.881 --> 00:16:09.985
our visions and our aspirations

00:16:09.986 --> 00:16:12.755
for what they'll be able
to be in our lives.

00:16:12.756 --> 00:16:14.550
But just the ability to build

00:16:14.551 --> 00:16:16.210
these systems in a
way that you can

00:16:16.211 --> 00:16:20.060
actually put them in the world
is hugely important.

00:16:20.061 --> 00:16:22.035
Even from a research standpoint,

00:16:22.036 --> 00:16:25.080
hugely important because
until these systems

00:16:25.081 --> 00:16:26.795
are in real environments

00:16:26.796 --> 00:16:28.140
where real people
interacting with them,

00:16:28.141 --> 00:16:29.820
you don't really know
what the phenomenon

00:16:29.821 --> 00:16:30.880
even is that you're trying

00:16:30.881 --> 00:16:33.990
to develop for and understand.

00:16:33.991 --> 00:16:38.390
So, that's been tremendous
and fairly recent.

00:16:38.391 --> 00:16:40.995
Even right now, I
would say, whatever,

00:16:40.996 --> 00:16:45.065
43 million Newton smart
speakers in people's homes now,

00:16:45.066 --> 00:16:48.720
people are living with AI in
a way they never did before,

00:16:48.721 --> 00:16:50.200
and who is interacting with

00:16:50.201 --> 00:16:51.740
AI is fundamentally different.

00:16:51.741 --> 00:16:53.670
It used to be just
like people like us.

00:16:53.671 --> 00:16:55.120
Now, it's young children

00:16:55.121 --> 00:16:57.535
and seniors and
everyone in-between,

00:16:57.536 --> 00:17:01.420
and we know very little
about how to design for

00:17:01.421 --> 00:17:03.590
the diversity of needs and

00:17:03.591 --> 00:17:07.045
opportunities of
such wide demographics.

00:17:07.046 --> 00:17:09.270
We still don't understand what

00:17:09.271 --> 00:17:11.725
this long-term relationship
is going to be.

00:17:11.726 --> 00:17:14.820
We build these very
transactional systems,

00:17:14.821 --> 00:17:15.930
but by and large.

00:17:15.931 --> 00:17:17.770
But I think the next level

00:17:17.771 --> 00:17:19.900
is kind of the Star Wars dream,

00:17:19.901 --> 00:17:22.705
robot sidekick,
helpful companion.

00:17:22.706 --> 00:17:24.140
Not just a digital assistant,

00:17:24.141 --> 00:17:28.000
but a helpful companion
that is with you

00:17:28.001 --> 00:17:29.999
and works with you
towards your goals

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:32.810
and really is your collaborator.

00:17:32.811 --> 00:17:36.260
That's still, I think,
where this is going.

00:17:36.261 --> 00:17:38.920
To me, that's like
a whole other layer

00:17:38.921 --> 00:17:40.090
beyond where we are right now.

00:17:40.091 --> 00:17:41.495
&gt;&gt; Yeah. You're absolutely right.

00:17:41.496 --> 00:17:45.070
So, I've been following

00:17:45.071 --> 00:17:46.975
your work over
the course of the time.

00:17:46.976 --> 00:17:49.395
One thing I've realized is that

00:17:49.396 --> 00:17:51.445
there is little bit of emphasis

00:17:51.446 --> 00:17:54.555
on building robots of

00:17:54.556 --> 00:17:56.450
the form such that you
can interact with them.

00:17:56.451 --> 00:17:57.800
For instance, they'll have eyes,

00:17:57.801 --> 00:18:00.690
they'll have some kind
of capability

00:18:00.691 --> 00:18:02.970
of making some kind of sound,

00:18:02.971 --> 00:18:05.990
expressions, et cetera.

00:18:05.991 --> 00:18:07.045
You contrast that to something

00:18:07.046 --> 00:18:09.560
like smart speakers
that you talked about.

00:18:09.561 --> 00:18:12.820
What role do you think such,

00:18:12.821 --> 00:18:14.910
for the lack of a better word,
I'm using the word of

00:18:14.911 --> 00:18:18.200
anthro and to more
feisty, for instance,

00:18:18.201 --> 00:18:21.595
basically, is important in

00:18:21.596 --> 00:18:25.180
establishing social
rapport with humans?

00:18:25.181 --> 00:18:26.815
&gt;&gt; Absolutely. Yeah, so I think

00:18:26.816 --> 00:18:33.155
the decades of science that's
been done in this field,

00:18:33.156 --> 00:18:34.435
there's been so many

00:18:34.436 --> 00:18:37.140
rigorous, randomized,
controlled trials,

00:18:37.141 --> 00:18:40.400
literally comparing
a physically embodied robot with

00:18:40.401 --> 00:18:43.660
various cues and affordances

00:18:43.661 --> 00:18:46.670
to graphical agents on a screen,

00:18:46.671 --> 00:18:48.440
to voice only agents,

00:18:48.441 --> 00:18:50.900
and now more recently,
to talking speakers.

00:18:50.901 --> 00:18:54.090
I think the data
and the evidence is

00:18:54.091 --> 00:18:57.110
pretty clear that
the co-physical,

00:18:57.111 --> 00:18:59.380
co-presence, social
emotional embodiment of

00:18:59.381 --> 00:19:01.330
the technology actually makes

00:19:01.331 --> 00:19:04.405
a significant difference in
how people engage with it.

00:19:04.406 --> 00:19:06.390
Again, it's just
speaking to the fact

00:19:06.391 --> 00:19:07.785
that we have evolved

00:19:07.786 --> 00:19:12.000
specialized neural
structures to do

00:19:12.001 --> 00:19:15.445
the social thinking and
inference with another that

00:19:15.446 --> 00:19:19.585
when you create a technology
that gives us those hooks,

00:19:19.586 --> 00:19:23.775
our minds engage with it in
a much richer, deeper way.

00:19:23.776 --> 00:19:27.175
So, if you can have
that depth of engagement,

00:19:27.176 --> 00:19:28.830
when people can invest more of

00:19:28.831 --> 00:19:30.740
themselves in
the multiple ways we

00:19:30.741 --> 00:19:32.850
have of understanding and

00:19:32.851 --> 00:19:34.730
reasoning and engaging
with something,

00:19:34.731 --> 00:19:36.010
not just cognitive but the

00:19:36.011 --> 00:19:38.660
social and emotional and
even the physical as well,

00:19:38.661 --> 00:19:42.150
the more successful often
we actually are because

00:19:42.151 --> 00:19:43.460
we're just able to put

00:19:43.461 --> 00:19:45.120
more of ourselves into
that interaction.

00:19:45.121 --> 00:19:46.980
I think that's one
of the key, I think,

00:19:46.981 --> 00:19:48.460
consistent insights from a lot

00:19:48.461 --> 00:19:50.440
of the work that's been done.

00:19:50.441 --> 00:19:53.290
Now, how human is
another question, right?

00:19:53.291 --> 00:19:55.600
So, I think this is
an important point

00:19:55.601 --> 00:19:57.320
because there's been kind of

00:19:57.321 --> 00:19:59.300
this assumption that the

00:19:59.301 --> 00:20:02.515
more plug compatible
human, the better.

00:20:02.516 --> 00:20:05.735
I actually don't think that's
panned out in the data.

00:20:05.736 --> 00:20:08.770
I think there is

00:20:08.771 --> 00:20:10.060
a different kind of

00:20:10.061 --> 00:20:13.795
relationship that people
can have with an other,

00:20:13.796 --> 00:20:15.340
that's a non-human other.

00:20:15.341 --> 00:20:17.200
So, you think about
companion animals, right?

00:20:17.201 --> 00:20:19.370
We can have meaningful,

00:20:19.371 --> 00:20:23.370
beneficial relationships
with non-human entities.

00:20:23.371 --> 00:20:26.820
In fact, one of the most
profound things, I think,

00:20:26.821 --> 00:20:28.930
about companion animals
is the fact that you can

00:20:28.931 --> 00:20:31.080
have this relationship that you

00:20:31.081 --> 00:20:34.020
feel is non-judgmental and

00:20:34.021 --> 00:20:36.470
it's just a different kind of
social, emotional support.

00:20:36.471 --> 00:20:40.730
So, that's just to say that
you can design a robot,

00:20:40.731 --> 00:20:44.675
a machine that does not
have to be human-like,

00:20:44.676 --> 00:20:47.550
to have actually
a really important kind

00:20:47.551 --> 00:20:48.710
of interaction relationship that

00:20:48.711 --> 00:20:50.710
provides tremendous value
to the person.

00:20:50.711 --> 00:20:53.420
So, we intentionally design

00:20:53.421 --> 00:20:56.970
our robots to be anthropomorphic,

00:20:56.971 --> 00:20:59.120
to be familiar enough
that it can tap into

00:20:59.121 --> 00:21:02.220
the social thinking processes
of the way that our brains

00:21:02.221 --> 00:21:04.190
are wired to work without

00:21:04.191 --> 00:21:06.225
trying to overstep and being too

00:21:06.226 --> 00:21:08.710
human because it actually
starts to set up

00:21:08.711 --> 00:21:11.560
a whole host of
expectations that,

00:21:11.561 --> 00:21:12.795
first of all, I think,

00:21:12.796 --> 00:21:14.655
we would not be able to uphold.

00:21:14.656 --> 00:21:17.250
Secondly, from
a philosophical standpoint,

00:21:17.251 --> 00:21:19.425
I feel like we've
got a lot of people,

00:21:19.426 --> 00:21:21.940
why do we want to make
robots look like people?

00:21:21.941 --> 00:21:23.855
It's like people are people.

00:21:23.856 --> 00:21:26.330
The intriguing design
challenge here

00:21:26.331 --> 00:21:28.460
is how to create
a technology that

00:21:28.461 --> 00:21:30.230
complements and dovetails and

00:21:30.231 --> 00:21:33.020
augments who we are
and how we do well.

00:21:33.021 --> 00:21:34.570
As opposed to trying to more-

00:21:34.571 --> 00:21:35.050
&gt;&gt; -would make us.

00:21:35.051 --> 00:21:36.980
&gt;&gt; -of what we are,
right? So, for me,

00:21:36.981 --> 00:21:38.800
that's a philosophical stance.

00:21:38.801 --> 00:21:40.670
But I've definitely
been finding that in

00:21:40.671 --> 00:21:46.600
creating non-human
anthropomorphic like robots

00:21:46.601 --> 00:21:49.700
has opened up huge opportunities

00:21:49.701 --> 00:21:51.850
for a different kind
of relationship,

00:21:51.851 --> 00:21:56.860
a far more humanistic experience

00:21:56.861 --> 00:21:58.820
and relationship with technology

00:21:58.821 --> 00:22:00.765
that actually empowers people.

00:22:00.766 --> 00:22:04.935
So, again, I think it's
one thing to use a useful tool,

00:22:04.936 --> 00:22:06.520
it's another thing
to interact with

00:22:06.521 --> 00:22:08.785
an empathetic supportive other.

00:22:08.786 --> 00:22:11.260
Different ways we
engage the world are

00:22:11.261 --> 00:22:13.040
unlocked when we engage

00:22:13.041 --> 00:22:14.530
with an empathetic
support of others.

00:22:14.531 --> 00:22:17.545
So, when you look at things
like around behavior change

00:22:17.546 --> 00:22:21.875
or learning something new
or aging with independence,

00:22:21.876 --> 00:22:23.420
these are all domains where

00:22:23.421 --> 00:22:25.230
the social and
emotional support are

00:22:25.231 --> 00:22:27.289
critical for our well-being,

00:22:27.290 --> 00:22:28.855
for our ability to flourish.

00:22:28.856 --> 00:22:31.350
We need to be able to
extend the kind of

00:22:31.351 --> 00:22:33.780
services and support
that people provide,

00:22:33.781 --> 00:22:37.645
either as family members
or as professionals.

00:22:37.646 --> 00:22:39.025
You may be able to get

00:22:39.026 --> 00:22:40.460
world-class kind of

00:22:40.461 --> 00:22:42.260
support and intervention
in the institution,

00:22:42.261 --> 00:22:44.090
but what's missing
now is we can't bring

00:22:44.091 --> 00:22:46.275
that same quality of
care to the home.

00:22:46.276 --> 00:22:48.535
So, when you think
about the opportunity

00:22:48.536 --> 00:22:51.025
of scalable, affordable,

00:22:51.026 --> 00:22:53.340
high-quality,
personalized services and

00:22:53.341 --> 00:22:55.890
support in the home,

00:22:55.891 --> 00:22:58.075
that's hugely empowering.

00:22:58.076 --> 00:22:59.480
I would love to have

00:22:59.481 --> 00:23:00.560
a personal tutor for

00:23:00.561 --> 00:23:02.205
my son living with us
and teach him Mandarin.

00:23:02.206 --> 00:23:04.065
I can't afford that, right?

00:23:04.066 --> 00:23:07.820
But with the right kind
of AI, that's possible.

00:23:07.821 --> 00:23:12.575
I think we just live in
a time where people just,

00:23:12.576 --> 00:23:15.760
we need help in

00:23:15.761 --> 00:23:18.460
order to just to be able to
fulfill our opportunity,

00:23:18.461 --> 00:23:20.930
to open up opportunity.

00:23:21.970 --> 00:23:24.745
To me, that's one of
the biggest promises

00:23:24.746 --> 00:23:26.010
of this technology.

00:23:26.011 --> 00:23:28.470
It's really supporting people

00:23:28.471 --> 00:23:33.450
to have the opportunity to
become who we aspire to be.

00:23:33.451 --> 00:23:34.755
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:23:34.756 --> 00:23:36.950
&gt;&gt; I think the democratization of

00:23:36.951 --> 00:23:38.470
that kind of services
and support is going

00:23:38.471 --> 00:23:41.730
to be critical, and
making that accessible

00:23:41.731 --> 00:23:45.255
to everyone to have
an AI- for AI,

00:23:45.256 --> 00:23:46.830
I think, to truly
fulfill its potential.

00:23:46.831 --> 00:23:47.500
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:23:47.501 --> 00:23:52.070
&gt;&gt; It needs to be able to
truly benefit everyone.

00:23:52.080 --> 00:23:54.270
As part of that, I feel it's

00:23:54.271 --> 00:23:55.470
not only enough that we try to

00:23:55.471 --> 00:23:58.590
design this kind of
humanistic AI with that in mind,

00:23:58.591 --> 00:24:01.715
but also we need to
empower children,

00:24:01.716 --> 00:24:04.540
people of all ages to understand
this technology, right?

00:24:04.541 --> 00:24:07.525
So, I'm a big advocate now of

00:24:07.526 --> 00:24:10.070
K12 AI education because children

00:24:10.071 --> 00:24:11.380
now are not just digital natives,

00:24:11.381 --> 00:24:12.270
they're AI natives, right?

00:24:12.271 --> 00:24:13.340
They're growing up
now, where they've

00:24:13.341 --> 00:24:15.450
always been able to talk
to intelligent machines.

00:24:15.451 --> 00:24:17.140
People don't understand
enough of what

00:24:17.141 --> 00:24:19.540
this technology is. They
need to understand that.

00:24:19.541 --> 00:24:20.820
They need to grow up
feeling empowered

00:24:20.821 --> 00:24:22.420
that they can create with it.

00:24:22.421 --> 00:24:24.765
So, I think this is
also really important.

00:24:24.766 --> 00:24:28.290
So, if only a small subset of

00:24:28.291 --> 00:24:31.800
a population is knowledgeable
enough to create solutions-

00:24:31.801 --> 00:24:32.130
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:24:32.131 --> 00:24:33.480
&gt;&gt; -by definition,
it's only going to

00:24:33.481 --> 00:24:35.090
serve a narrow part of

00:24:35.091 --> 00:24:36.710
the population and
that's going to

00:24:36.711 --> 00:24:39.220
exacerbate any divide,
not close it.

00:24:39.221 --> 00:24:42.125
So, I think a lot about
the democratization

00:24:42.126 --> 00:24:45.370
of AI on how you empower many,

00:24:45.371 --> 00:24:48.130
many more people to
create solutions of

00:24:48.131 --> 00:24:50.355
personal significance
through this technology.

00:24:50.356 --> 00:24:52.470
&gt;&gt; Yeah. When you talk

00:24:52.471 --> 00:24:56.005
about AI fulfilling
this ambitious goal,

00:24:56.006 --> 00:25:00.630
one of the things that occurs
to me is that we are asking

00:25:00.631 --> 00:25:05.280
this technology to actually
do many things well together.

00:25:05.281 --> 00:25:06.950
It's being about, say,

00:25:06.951 --> 00:25:10.170
a speech recognition, or
recognize social cues, or take

00:25:10.171 --> 00:25:12.100
actions, or help us drive

00:25:12.101 --> 00:25:14.695
on the streets safely,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:25:14.696 --> 00:25:18.360
But as a species we excel at

00:25:18.361 --> 00:25:21.910
engineering where there was
very well-defined goal.

00:25:21.911 --> 00:25:23.910
For instance, let's build cars,

00:25:23.911 --> 00:25:26.020
let's build bridges,
let's build airplanes,

00:25:26.021 --> 00:25:28.000
but now we talk
multi technology that

00:25:28.001 --> 00:25:30.585
needs to do many things well.

00:25:30.586 --> 00:25:33.340
So, from a design standpoint,

00:25:33.341 --> 00:25:35.370
how do you think about
building such technologies?

00:25:35.371 --> 00:25:36.545
Are the certain principles

00:25:36.546 --> 00:25:38.330
in your world that you'd

00:25:38.331 --> 00:25:41.470
have found out that

00:25:41.471 --> 00:25:43.700
we should be aware of and we
should think about those?

00:25:43.701 --> 00:25:45.415
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, I would definitely,

00:25:45.416 --> 00:25:46.640
in the nature of my work.

00:25:46.641 --> 00:25:48.720
So, the core of my work
is really about

00:25:48.721 --> 00:25:51.150
how do you create the
human-machine partnership, right?

00:25:51.151 --> 00:25:54.925
That, I think, the core
fundamentals that cut across

00:25:54.926 --> 00:25:58.995
any sort of purpose or
problem to be solved,

00:25:58.996 --> 00:26:02.660
is what nature of
intelligence and capability

00:26:02.661 --> 00:26:04.600
the machine need to have

00:26:04.601 --> 00:26:06.620
to support this
human partnership.

00:26:06.621 --> 00:26:10.930
There's a lot we can learn
from psychology around

00:26:10.931 --> 00:26:14.430
the role of these
non-verbal cues, to how you

00:26:14.431 --> 00:26:17.500
infer goals and
intense and desires,

00:26:17.501 --> 00:26:19.220
and how you get
that from language,

00:26:19.221 --> 00:26:21.470
all of these kind of, again,

00:26:21.471 --> 00:26:23.420
the social and emotional
intelligence of the machine.

00:26:23.421 --> 00:26:25.450
That is a very rich,

00:26:25.451 --> 00:26:27.880
relatively very under
explored area of AI,

00:26:27.881 --> 00:26:29.500
but I think that's the foundation

00:26:29.501 --> 00:26:30.900
that cuts across all of it.

00:26:30.901 --> 00:26:34.090
If you want to create
collaborative technologies

00:26:34.091 --> 00:26:35.390
that empower people.

00:26:35.391 --> 00:26:38.160
Then, you get down to
the specific purposes, right?

00:26:38.161 --> 00:26:39.430
There may be multiple purposes,

00:26:39.431 --> 00:26:40.510
but then there's
a whole other host

00:26:40.511 --> 00:26:41.670
of design decisions you may

00:26:41.671 --> 00:26:45.200
make depending on how focused
or broad that needs to be.

00:26:45.201 --> 00:26:47.160
So, there's, I think, a core set

00:26:47.161 --> 00:26:49.110
of social emotional
intelligence challenges that,

00:26:49.111 --> 00:26:51.340
again, we're still in
our infancy of understanding.

00:26:51.341 --> 00:26:54.235
Then, there's understanding
how that intersects with

00:26:54.236 --> 00:26:57.160
the affordances of the utility

00:26:57.161 --> 00:26:59.990
and function that, which would
have influence the nature,

00:26:59.991 --> 00:27:01.730
the embodiment and the sensors,

00:27:01.731 --> 00:27:03.670
and the other kind of
knowledge in machine

00:27:03.671 --> 00:27:05.890
learning algorithm that
would go into creating that.

00:27:05.891 --> 00:27:10.410
But I think, when we
were graduate students,

00:27:10.411 --> 00:27:12.660
I think it may still be the case.

00:27:12.661 --> 00:27:14.440
There was this bias that

00:27:14.441 --> 00:27:16.675
system integration
wasn't interested,

00:27:16.676 --> 00:27:18.140
that building these systems,

00:27:18.141 --> 00:27:21.050
that wasn't research, that
was just engineering.

00:27:21.051 --> 00:27:23.400
That the science was
something different, right?

00:27:23.401 --> 00:27:25.400
I think now, maybe we're

00:27:25.401 --> 00:27:28.170
starting to deeply
understand that

00:27:28.171 --> 00:27:31.425
building the whole
brain-body system

00:27:31.426 --> 00:27:33.825
is actually really hard research.

00:27:33.826 --> 00:27:35.920
Really, really hard research.

00:27:35.921 --> 00:27:37.605
We've got to start thinking about

00:27:37.606 --> 00:27:39.490
the integration of all of

00:27:39.491 --> 00:27:40.470
these things, rather
than thinking

00:27:40.471 --> 00:27:41.490
we can go deep and narrow,

00:27:41.491 --> 00:27:42.820
went to core
capacities and think,

00:27:42.821 --> 00:27:44.565
"You just stick it
together, and that's all."

00:27:44.566 --> 00:27:46.465
That's, like, no. No, no, no, no.

00:27:46.466 --> 00:27:48.660
That's not the case. Then, you

00:27:48.661 --> 00:27:50.095
have to put it in the world,

00:27:50.096 --> 00:27:51.060
and now you see how

00:27:51.061 --> 00:27:52.300
people engage with
it and then there's

00:27:52.301 --> 00:27:53.560
a whole other host of learners

00:27:53.561 --> 00:27:54.865
that thinks you've
got to figure out.

00:27:54.866 --> 00:27:58.255
So, I mean, we live
in interesting times.

00:27:58.256 --> 00:28:00.590
&gt;&gt; Yeah. No. You know,

00:28:00.591 --> 00:28:04.380
the whole area of robotics'
social and emotional robots,

00:28:04.381 --> 00:28:05.670
as you said, it's

00:28:05.671 --> 00:28:07.940
incredibly difficult
enduring challenge.

00:28:07.941 --> 00:28:09.880
So, one final parting question,

00:28:09.881 --> 00:28:12.215
one final question that I
wanted to ask you is that,

00:28:12.216 --> 00:28:15.320
for someone who aspires
to be in this field,

00:28:15.321 --> 00:28:16.670
who wants to work with robotics,

00:28:16.671 --> 00:28:17.800
who wants to work about,

00:28:17.801 --> 00:28:20.535
think about the society

00:28:20.536 --> 00:28:22.805
and affect of systems,
and things like those.

00:28:22.806 --> 00:28:26.165
The barrier to entry right
now is quite high, right?

00:28:26.166 --> 00:28:28.510
In the spirit of
democratizing, for instance,

00:28:28.511 --> 00:28:30.350
AI and machine learning
to the world,

00:28:30.351 --> 00:28:34.740
how an aspiring student
should think about?

00:28:34.741 --> 00:28:36.790
Do you have any advices,

00:28:36.791 --> 00:28:38.705
any word of advice for them?

00:28:38.706 --> 00:28:40.425
&gt;&gt; Yeah. I think-

00:28:40.426 --> 00:28:43.625
So, that's also why I'm
so passionate about

00:28:43.626 --> 00:28:47.050
even pre-K12 AI education
because I want to

00:28:47.051 --> 00:28:50.650
make these experiences
accessible for many,

00:28:50.651 --> 00:28:52.860
many more kids much earlier on.

00:28:52.861 --> 00:28:54.160
I think, right now,

00:28:54.161 --> 00:28:57.285
based on when we were
kids, we had Lego, right?

00:28:57.286 --> 00:28:59.950
Now, there is Mindstorms
and there's Scratch,

00:28:59.951 --> 00:29:01.260
and there's all these other kinds

00:29:01.261 --> 00:29:02.920
of ways that kids can
actually get into

00:29:02.921 --> 00:29:04.740
coding and programming
and interacting with

00:29:04.741 --> 00:29:07.150
the technology,
which is phenomenal.

00:29:07.151 --> 00:29:08.880
We need to bring it into schools.

00:29:08.881 --> 00:29:10.070
That needs to come into our

00:29:10.071 --> 00:29:12.660
K-12 formal education process.

00:29:12.661 --> 00:29:14.700
There's movement to try to
bring computer science,

00:29:14.701 --> 00:29:17.715
and with that I think we want
to bring in AI education.

00:29:17.716 --> 00:29:19.220
I just think that's
important just to make

00:29:19.221 --> 00:29:20.955
the opportunity
available to people.

00:29:20.956 --> 00:29:24.370
There are a lot of
after-school activities.

00:29:24.371 --> 00:29:26.720
There are a number of
products on the market.

00:29:26.721 --> 00:29:28.730
There's a lot of things
you can do online.

00:29:28.731 --> 00:29:32.430
I think the good news
is there's way

00:29:32.431 --> 00:29:34.150
that you can start to get

00:29:34.151 --> 00:29:36.660
your hands dirty with
this kind of stuff.

00:29:36.661 --> 00:29:38.730
What we really want
to do is just make

00:29:38.731 --> 00:29:44.620
that richer and more
empowering, I think,

00:29:44.621 --> 00:29:47.880
for kids to be able
to apply that,

00:29:47.881 --> 00:29:49.725
honestly at all ages,

00:29:49.726 --> 00:29:51.250
to things that matter to

00:29:51.251 --> 00:29:53.030
them because I think
that's when you really

00:29:53.031 --> 00:29:54.660
start to develop empathy and you

00:29:54.661 --> 00:29:56.830
start to appreciate that creating

00:29:56.831 --> 00:29:59.520
solutions is not just this field

00:29:59.521 --> 00:30:00.935
or that field or the other,

00:30:00.936 --> 00:30:04.125
but it's how they
all come together,

00:30:04.126 --> 00:30:05.590
and we're both from
the media lab.

00:30:05.591 --> 00:30:07.640
So, we're already commerce

00:30:07.641 --> 00:30:09.740
of multi-disciplinary thinking.

00:30:09.741 --> 00:30:12.330
But to build real things

00:30:12.331 --> 00:30:14.400
that will impact
real people's lives,

00:30:14.401 --> 00:30:16.390
the insights and the ways of

00:30:16.391 --> 00:30:19.000
understanding across
these sub-disciplines,

00:30:19.001 --> 00:30:20.765
they have to come together.

00:30:20.766 --> 00:30:22.920
That's when you really come
up with the solutions.

00:30:22.921 --> 00:30:24.710
I think that makes sense.

00:30:24.711 --> 00:30:26.470
But building that empathy
and the ability to

00:30:26.471 --> 00:30:28.160
have those conversations and

00:30:28.161 --> 00:30:30.040
the appreciation of these
different ways of thinking and

00:30:30.041 --> 00:30:32.815
understanding is also
really important.

00:30:32.816 --> 00:30:35.855
&gt;&gt; Yeah. Great. Thank
you for the good time.

00:30:35.856 --> 00:30:37.350
I enjoyed the conversation.

00:30:37.351 --> 00:30:39.270
&gt;&gt; Yeah. Thank you.

